00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
now rail passengers face further travel  disruption today as staff go on strike  for a third time this week because of a  dispute over pay and job security  joining me now is the general secretary  of the rmt union mick lynch mr good  morning to you thank you so much for  talking to us so where are we with these  talks  well we're constantly engaged with the  companies we've had sessions of  discussions with them all week  we haven't made the advances that we'd  like to make but we will still keep  engaging with them they're putting their  ideas to us  they are along the lines that we've been  telling people that they want to make  very severe changes to our members terms  and conditions they do want to cut  thousands of jobs they want to  re-contract virtually everyone that  works on the railway on a set of terms  and additions and pay that is lower  than we currently have and that in some  ways is a form of fire and rehire  they're saying we either have to adapt  and adopt these new practices  or we will lose our jobs in the greater  numbers  than we even we thought so there is  still that constant threat to us  of mass job losses thousands of jobs  new contracts of employment new working  practices which will be detrimental to  our people  and we still haven't got any offer  that's suitable on pay  and most of our people haven't had a pay  rise for two or three years  because we've spoken to the network rail  and they have said that we had talks  again this morning this was yesterday  we're really getting now into the  nitty-gritty of the reforms we want to  see and how much money that that can  unlock to convert into a better pay deal  on the table so we are making progress  it is inch by inch though rather than  foot by foot so do you agree with brie  with that there is progress being made  in these talks  well we are getting to down to what they  actually want they're giving us a lot of  detail about what they want from  what they might call the new modern  railway what we don't know is how our  members going to respond to that we hear  a lot of  a lot of the changes they want to make  but our members when they hear it will  find a lot of these changes very  difficult  to take on board so we'll have to see  what the complete package is and then  we'll have to go to our people  and consult them in detail to see if  they want to accept this package  so there's a long way to go yet  but most of the stuff that our members  voted  very heavily in favor and for action  about  are what's on the table now and they've  not diluted  very much the stuff that they want and  that's true of the train operators and  network rail and we have to make sure  that we keep both of these things moving  together we don't want one of the one  section of the railway in advance of the  other and we are determined to get a  settlement for all 43 000 people in this  dispute  so where we are today this morning do  you think we're going to have more  strikes  well at the moment we will have strikes  because we've we've got  have not got an offer on the three  elements of the deal we've got  job security which means uh thousands of  job cuts and that's still on the table  we've got these working practices and  conditions which as i've said are very  difficult to absorb and we haven't got a  pay deal and we've now got another  element  uh on the mainline railway in the train  operating companies about pension cuts  and pension reforms and we've also got  that issue on london underground so it's  likely  unless we get a lot of movement  provided by the government that the  companies can change their stance that  there will be more action yes and we've  got a funding crisis at the bottom of  this  all of these disputes are about a two  billion pound cup on the mainline  railway and a two billion pound cut on  transport for london and that is what's  difficult to swallow because if you cut  that amount of money out of the system  the worker's going to be worse off and  that's what the situation we're facing  and we've got to deal with that and so  it's a tough set of negotiations to go  i'm sorry if talks are happening and  even if there is progress only inch by  inch and everyone's saying there is some  progress being made then why strike why  disrupt  the entire nation with this  well we haven't named any strike action  at the moment this is the strike action  we  call to get the companies to the table  but what you've got to remember what  they're calling progress is them taking  money off our members to a large extent  any pay offer  that they make to us which is only in  the region of three percent at the  moment and inflation is rampant in  double figures at the moment will be  funded by  job losses and changes to working  practices and to a large extent  re-contracting staff on lower wages  so it's not as if we're getting loads of  money off these companies they're  actually offering lower money  uh into the system and taking money out  of the system rather than putting money  into it usually when you go into pay  talks you're looking for new money to  come on the table and that's not the  case at the moment so it's going to be  very difficult for us to get a  settlement and if they do what they said  last week that they're actually going to  start making the redundancies  that will harden our position and if  they threaten our members jobs we will  take more action  again back to what network rail said  they said that they  they need to have a modern railway um  modernizing it and modernizing  maintenance means that the workforce  will shrink but that they believe they  can make most of the changes without  forcing anyone out of a job  well they can make changes for 12 years  network rail gave us a guarantee of no  compulsory redundancies they withdrew  that  last year in preparation for this  dispute because they were getting ready  for it what we need them to do is commit  to no compulsory redundancies and then  we can  discuss with them this modern railway  which we are already doing  and the flexibilities they're looking  for but that can't completely disrupt  our members lives and their work-life  balance we want our members to be able  to see their families and live in their  communities what they're offering at the  moment is almost constant nights and  constant weekend working at night  and that will change our members lives  and they simply don't get rewarded at  level they're not night workers the pay  and the salaries is very modest and what  they want in return for the check what  they're giving in return for the changes  they're proposing is not adequate  and i'm not convinced at the moment that  our members will accept that so we've  got to be very cautious  about what they call progress there may  be progress in their agenda but it  doesn't mean that our members going to  accept those changes just because the  company wants them so we've got to work  that problem through with them we will  do that as constructive  uh  in a constructive dialogue but it's  still a long way to go  if you don't think  if you don't think your members are  going to accept  what they've got on the table it sounds  like you do think there are going to be  more strikes  throughout this summer i mean do you  have days  that you are looking to have more action  when do you think that would be we have  we have not selected any days but the  whole point of a dialogue in a  negotiation is that people change their  position  and you get to a new position that  doesn't belong to either party  so that you can form a constructive way  forward now at the moment  the companies have given us all the  reforms they want but they're not  listening  fully to what we need and what our  members need to get a decent  working life  on the railway and that's got to change  they need to change their position  in terms of what they're offering in  terms of pay what they can do for job  security and the way that they engage  their staff  and come to agreement with us about the  way people work so we'll work with with  them on that we've not named dates we're  going to  review where we are in the discussions  next week and then we will decide  if we need to take more action  so that decision will come next week on  whether or not you're going to take more  action well i haven't said that what i  said is we'll review where we are next  week we'll see where the discussions are  going we'll allow time for more  discussions and then we'll decide what  we're going to do when we've consulted  our members so no strikes until then  say that again no more strikes until  then  well we have to give two weeks notice of  strikes anyway that's the legislation so  there won't be any strikes in the in you  know in the next couple of weeks but  we'll decide that but we won't hesitate  to use more industrial action if we  can't reach an agreement or if the  companies carry through their threats to  make people redundant network rail  issued a notice a formal letter of  redundancy  last week that's entirely unacceptable  as a step to take in the middle of a  negotiation that we won't take those  threats  lightly so we need to get on with  constructive dialogue and that's the  best way to avoid more industrial action  by reaching a settlement that's  that suits both parties needs  okay mr lynch thank you so much for your  time this morning
 

